  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 406 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nConsensus in global solutions keep showing a potential low \ndeveloping along a stalled frontal boundary around NE Florida, which \nthen slowly migrates northward across the W Atlantic during the \nfirst half of the week. Weak southerly flow will combine with the \ninfluence of the low to support tropical moisture advection across \nthe state. NBM POPs remain in the 70-80% range each day through the \nend of the work week. Coverage becomes widespread across SoFlo by \nTuesday, with a generally wet weather pattern prevailing through the \nlong term. \n\nAs the W Atlantic ridge axis pushes further east, pressure gradients \nacross SoFlo will weaken and bring winds across to a light flow or \neven calm at times. This will allow for afternoon sea breeze and \nstorm outflow boundaries to drive storm development and motion. Main \nhazards will include damaging gusts, lightning strikes, and \nlocalized flooding.  \n\nHigh pressure should then re-establish in the wake of the \nlow/front for the start of the weekend, bringing back a more \ntypical easterly wind regime, along with decreasing POPs/Wx \ncoverage. \n\nTemperatures will remain hot each afternoon with highs in the upper \n80s to low 90s, along with heat index values in the low-mid 100s.",
